      "text": "While chora can mean \"land\" or \"city,\" when used in the context of the polis it more often means the surrounding countryside; thus, a polis actually consists of a town (asty) and territory (chora) (McEwen 155n4; see also Sallis 116). Looking at the archaeological work ofFrant,ois de Polignac, McEwen describes the placement of sanctuaries in early Greek cities as falling into three areas, with some placed within the inhabited urban area; others established at short distances from the urban area (suburban): and still others located at the limits of the city's territory (chora), some six to twelve kilometers away from the center. McEwen argues that here we see Uthe notion of apolis allowed to appear as a surface woven by the activity of its inhabitants\" in \"ritual processions from center to urban limit to territorial limit and back again~ (81). The movements from city center to outlands and back constitute the weaving of the city whereby what is constructed\n\nemerges directly from the situated activity of the inhabitants, much like the dance \"weaves\" the dance floor.\n\nOf particular relevance is the affinity that shows up here between architectural and discursive construction. Both require some sense of place for instantiation to occur, but instantiation itselfresults from spatial dynamics. If in its more archaic sense the chora was a territory made to appear through what McEwen characterizes as a \"continual remaking or reweaving of its encompassing surface\" (82), an instability becomes apparent in the notion of the polis. It is always bumping up against a limit or boundary that it must exceed while retaining a dependency it wants to overcome. The movements beyond the city's boundaries mark the weaving of the city because the polis cannot thrive without them. In this regard, McEwen remarks that Plato's Republic and Laws are striking in their claim that political order can be thought without this sense of making or weaving integral to the polis (98). From this perspective, the Republic is stillborn, even ifit stems from an engagement with Athens itself, because it utilizes ideas that remain theoretical, since they lack a place from which they can thrive. However, as the Timaeus makes clear, similar misgivings must also have gripped Plato insofar as he attempts to move from the Republic's static ideals-a theory of the perfect city-to a spatial dynamic for those ideals that can move from dialogic discussion to mythical surrogate (Atlantis) to actualization as Athens itself. Crucially, for Plato, the ch6ra takes on far greater import as well, even if at base it is a means to explain how the (eternal, perfect) Forms become individuated beings. There are political ramifications, for instance. A ch6ra is not just the outlying territory on which the city depends. Rather, it becomes a fundamental cosmological principle, the receptacle (hypodochen), the matrix (or womb, metra), for all that comes to be. The import granted the city reminds us that the social is never forgotten in the question of the material. They go together. For things to be what they are, for formal ideals to actualize themselves in experience, they must be illuminated by everyday being. The generative and orchestrating background that gives place remains in its giving.\n\nThe Matter of Beginning in Plato's Timeaus: Ch6ra Ch6ra Choral\n\nFor all this, the Platonic ch6ra remains murky. As Timaeus says, the ch6ra participates in the intelligible (noetou) in a manner most perplexing and baffling (aporotata) (Timaeus s1a-b). This ambiguity helps explain why Aristotle's far clearer assimilation of chora to hyle (matter) and",
