      "text": "The Timaeus thereby stages for us a new kind of beginning, one that moves from the realm of the idea to the world of generation, or from static being to vital becoming. This is what Socrates and his comrades desire to generate. and the purpose of the Timaeus is to bring the ideal polis to life as an actual city, one that has a place. Eventually this place will come to correspond with Athens. In making this comparison between Athens and the ideal city, the Timaeus implicitly argues that a beginning is tied to place and memory and that both beginnings and place are woven through the polis. Further, the choric city will be one that not only has a place but encompasses eros. The limits to fabrication can be exceeded through productive eros, an idea that adds to the implicit importance of place a bodily dimension (as well as a reinscription of a maternal feminine within masculinist production, a point Kristeva underscores). In other words, the choric city is where invention comes to life precisely because the chora reworks the distinction between techne andphysis that Plato seems to assume ash is starting point. The chora thus provides Plato a means to explain the movement from Idea to becoming as a form of vital, robust actuality. This relation parallels the connection McEwen sees between dancing and having a place to dance: to give something a place means to see it in action and vice versa. Put differently, human activity is already enworlded. This helps explain the import of the legend about ancient Athens facing off against the Atlante ans. While the Athens of Plato's day appears less noble in comparison, the Athens of old nevertheless comes to embody not just the ideal polis discussed in the Republic but also those ideals further ennobled in agonistic activity.\n\nDespite these insights and explanations, something about the ch6ra remains elusive. resisting determination (see Sallis 3-6). This problem is already noted in the Timaeus. Timaeus tells us that the ch6ra is \"most dif-\n\nficult to catch\" and apprehensible only by means of a \"bastard reasoning\" (51a-b, 52b). The chora is of a third kind that is not really a \"kind\"; it is a receptacle (hypodoche), which Edward Casey describes as something \"at once locatory and yet not itselflocated, permanent and yet invisible, underlying and yet insubstantial\" (37). The chora is the matrix of all becoming, yet it declines to be determined, and in this sense, it is not strictly speaking a Form. As Casey argues, then, while ch6ra is not a thing, \"it is a locatory matrix/or things\" (34), It is what is necessary for the genesis of things, that in which (en ho) and from which (ex hou) they show up and pass away; but the ch6ra also recedes, declining to leave its imprint on things just as it declines to take on the qualities of the things it receives (Timaeus soc). There is a dichotomy between what occurs in the passage to actuality by means of which things show up and their actual showing up. A beginning, even as something unstable or retroactively posited, is never equivalent to what has emerged.\n\nPlato thus deploys the ch6ra to open a theme to which I will return throughout this book, that ambience can never be understood simply as presence. Place is not simply an immediate environment; it includes the background by means of which things show up as what they are. Our everyday environment coheres, but it is choreographed in advance, as it were, so that its particularity is hollowed out beforehand. Such a background recedes, or withdraws, even as it generates. In this sense placing occurs through displacing, a point Derrida makes by showing how the ch6ra disrupts representation itself (and hence rhetoric, too, if it is conceived in terms of symbolicity) even while it remains fundamental for the passage to representation.\n\nThough the Timaeus sometimes waxes large with cosmological import, the older sense of ch6ra as \"city\" or \"land\" remains present. The dialogue's attempt to wrestle with the limitations of fabrication (or techne) following the discussion of the ideal city invites us to consider the relation of the chora to the polis and of the polis to its exterior or what lies outside its boundaries. Recall that the region surrounding and sustaining a polis is also called ch6ra. Thus, as was mentioned with respect to McEwen, the chora has a specifically political dimension, being both the boundary of the city and what lies beyond the boundar y. What must be underscored here is the necessity for the polis to go beyond its boundaries to thrive (a reinforcement ofTimaeus's anagke at 47e). These patterns of boundary and disruption weave throughout the Timaeus. For example, the fact that approaching",
