    {
      "raw_rank": 5,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00170",
      "distance": 1.1148271560668945,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 284,
      "page_end": 286,
      "text": "island, not a resource for the maximization of human uses and profits. In their way oflife they give the island to take part, not to preserve it or to naturalize it, but to cultivate it as part of their homemaking. Indeed, this gets at the essence of dwelling, caring for as a freeing to let something be what it is.14 Rather than utter no to technology or to building, this hearkening brings forth a relation to technology that takes its bearings from the profundity of a holistic ecology, which tempers the will to impose, dominate, or master with the call to care.\n\nThe world, thought as material presence, as meaning, and as that which withdraws, is sacralized by everyday practices in such a fashion as to ground meaning and worth beyond the human. Values, ifwe can still risk that term, emerge from these practices and are neither settled nor projected solely by human beings. On this account, if values in this sense transcend human being, then Latour's Dingpolitik must already be spoken in a way oflife. Dwelling gives rise to Dingpolitik, which is to say that there is already something like an ethic implicit in our worldliness. There is no adding on a new comportment toward things, toward world after the fact, as Latour would have it. Indeed, this is the crucial point for understanding why religion, art, and other forms of knowledge are just as important as science or politics; they are already in deep relation to and disclosive for our way of being, for the way we dwell. for ethos. Thus, performing rhetoric in an ambient key is less a matter of adding them back in than of reattuning ourselves to their station, and to what their station may yet be, in our struggle to find better, more sustainable practices. Certainly, their role in knowledge formation and ethics remains vital. But there are other aspects that could be made equally vital. For instance, religion, art, and forms of celebration would be important for their evocation of something like the festive. Festivity need not be happy and chipper, either; the festive is important for attuning us to the gifting of the world and a sense of value that transcends human being, manifest through moods of contemplation, reverence, joy, or other possibilities.15 Such celebration is also a form of preparatory rhetoric, since it discloses world differently and opens the possibility of dwelling anew. That is, art, religion, and other ways of being with things would allow them to show up for us as things that are indispensable for our way oflife and for the being of world in general: they are radiant in such fashion that they are graced to us and so lay claims on us should we listen to them. 16 Note that this aligns with Latour's and Heidegger's claims that science is not the sole source for knowledge about things. One need not\n\naccede to antiscience religious doxa to uphold the idea that science is not the last instance for determining the knowledge that enlivens our everyday being. The festive is one way to pursue this enlivening.\n\nAlthough I find the Toronto Islanders' resistance to the automobile important and illuminating, it is important not to romanticize their efforts, as if the island's residents have found an ideal path that we should then imitate. Not only is it difficult to transport practices that are so wedded to the region, since the specificity of place is an integral element in those practices, but it is far from assured that they have succeeded. Indeed, I have spent a good amount oftime explicating where they struggle. Nevertheless, we cannot neglect the rhetorical potency of arguments such as Princen's or practices such as those of the Toronto Island residents. I have stated that they are only preparatory, and I want to close on that note. Such preparation lays the groundwork necessary for awakening attunements that in turn give rise to other forms of disclosure and receptivity. That is, thought in terms of an ambient rhetoric, such preparatory work is crucial, for it brings us in touch with the world's primordial affectability, and disclosing this transforms how world comes to us in a way that shifts our manner of being in that world. This suggests that this book itself is ultimately only preparatory. Perhaps this too is necessary. An ambient rhetoric cannot trump itself, cannot short-circuit itself into being a new paradigm. That would run counter to its disclosive power for rhetoric itself. When it comes to world making, an ambient rhetoric can only attune and, by such attunements, conjoin us in worldly affectability giving place and bearing to everything we are and everything we do.\n\nMovement, Heidegger's Silence, Disclosure",
