    {
      "raw_rank": 8,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00063",
      "distance": 1.1506264209747314,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 111,
      "page_end": 112,
      "text": "We can draw still more conclusions from Dourish's example. First, while we cannot say that the controllers, aircraft, instrumentation, strips, knowledge, discourses, and work activity constitute an abstract unity, we equally cannot say that anything here resembles poles, middles, or-a point I made in regard to Consigny and to which I will return at the end of this chapter-irrationality. The controllers are not just adapting to an environment, but (despite their job designation) they are not simply controlling it, either. The air control center is a series of events in a specific environs, ofkairotic moments in a generative place, that form an ambient whole. Elements are individuated in practical activity from this ambient whole while still related to all the other elements; this complex, relied-on backdrop of relations constitutes the place as place. The immediate environment radically distributes the activities of the subjectivities working within it, even as particular aspects do emerge through discrete practices. No \"subject controls\" what occurs; rather, actions emerge as willed by the situation precisely because there are no discrete subjects absent their relations and connections. However, the environs here are not just a material reality to which we adapt or a material situation that somehow \"determines\" us. Instead, the environs enable, but they enable inclusively ofhuman beings insofar as human beings take shape within the environs. Thus, the emplacement of the controllers is essential to their activity, for the context makes all that occurs possible. The point is not that the controllers do not make choices but that their choices are already immersed in the context in which they get played out. On Dourish's account, then, to deny the fundamentally constitutive role of place is to suggest that somehow we are back to a subjectobject relationship, with a priori discrete elements whose connection must be explained across a fundamental gap, a position that drags us yet again into the depressing morass of subjectivism in an objective world.\n\nLet me return to the example of Gorgias's asking an audience for a topic\n\nand see whether Dourish's argument concerning air traffic controllers can teach us anything. Ifwe keep to a subjective understanding of the world, whereby this request for a topic casts Gorgias as a rhetor playing out his skill for an objective audience, then it might make sense to see him as a master of knowledge performing his mastery. Ifwe work out of a different conception, such that we are beings already being together in the world, with world taking on a more richly materialist modality than, say, Consigny allows, then it would make better sense to say that Gorgias is responding to a dynamic material situation and kairotically inviting the crowd in as coinventor oftl1e speech about to ensue. This reading of Gorgias, albeit in a different idiom, has already been suggested by Dale Sullivan, who sees the moment as one of an originary kairotic connection between speaker and audience (325; see also Muckelbauer 118). That is, rather than act as a master of knowledge, Gorgias to varying degrees offloads rhetorical work onto the crowd, who are gathered there in a material forum that also contributes to the experience.18 This forum may include a water clock that shapes the temporal fl.ow of the rhetorical performance and is thus integral to any characterization of the overall situation. A caveat: of course there is little way of knowing how Gorgias really experienced and understood his performance. Few words from or reports about him remain extant, and the concerns, material and otherwise, of our own era inevitably contribute to the way Gorgias discloses himself to us through the tradition we have inherited. Still, I have been attempting to demonstrate that, contrary to the dominant readings ofGorgias and kairos, the extant writings provide enough evidence to warrant proposing fresh theoretical insight. An ambient account does not \"rob• Gorgias of the skill for which he is recognized. either; however, it does require us to rethink what is entailed in rhetorical skill. I am not, that is, simply reading otherwise. There is more to this material than has hitherto been thought. Nor have I exhausted it. What I hope to have demonstrated is that an ontological form ofkairos, materially emplaced and dispersed, was, in some form of disclosure, available to or at least implicit in ancient Greek thought. What remains next for me, then, is to provide an extended example showing what this might mean for kairotic invention in rhetorical theory today and in the future.\n\nKairos and Ambient Invention in The Usual Suspects",
      "meta": {
        "source_method": "marker+bbox",
